    <description>
      <Value><![CDATA[{
  "blocks": {
    "contentData": [],
    "settingsData": [],
    "expose": [],
    "Layout": {}
  },
  "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EWhere the elements of the existing urban grain are not positive or are fragmented, applicants \u003Cstrong\u003Eshould \u003C/strong\u003Erefer to the historic urban grain and look to restore this (e.g. where there is existing cul-de-sac development in an area that was historically more linear). \u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EException:\u003C/strong\u003E Where no historic urban grain is available applicants \u003Cstrong\u003Eshould \u003C/strong\u003Econsider: \u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003Edesigning a layout that responds to existing natural features like topography, waterways, and vegetation to create a sense of place \u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003Eusing principles of human-scale design to ensure walkability, clear routes, and well\u0002defined public and private spaces \u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003Eapplying best practice in urban design to establish a legible street network with varied building forms and rhythms that encourage community interaction\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003C/p\u003E"
}]]></Value>
    </description>
